WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:02.540
The full title of this video might be something like

00:00:02.540 --> 00:00:08.620
"How pretty but pointless patterns in polar plots of primes prompt pretty important ponderings on properties of those primes"

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:17.600
I first saw this pattern that I'm about to show you in a question on the Math Stack Exchange

00:00:17.640 --> 00:00:22.020
It was asked by a user under the name "dwymark" and answered by Greg Martin.

00:00:22.020 --> 00:00:23.980
And it relates to the distribution of prime numbers

00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:26.720
together with rational approximations for pi.

00:00:27.180 --> 00:00:31.700
You see what the user had been doing was playing around with data in polar coordinates.

00:00:31.700 --> 00:00:33.680
As a quick reminder so we're all in the same page:

00:00:34.040 --> 00:00:38.404
This means labeling points in 2D space, not with the usual xy coordinates,

00:00:38.404 --> 00:00:40.940
but instead with a distance from the origin,

00:00:41.680 --> 00:00:47.100
commonly called r for radius together with the angle that that radial line makes with the horizontal

00:00:47.560 --> 00:00:49.380
commonly called theta.

00:00:49.380 --> 00:00:52.520
And for our purposes this angle will be measured in radians.

00:00:52.880 --> 00:00:56.720
Which basically means that an angle of π is halfway around

00:00:57.020 --> 00:00:59.460
and then 2π is a full circle.

00:01:00.540 --> 00:01:03.780
And notice polar coordinates are not unique in the sense that

00:01:03.780 --> 00:01:09.320
adding 2π to that second coordinate doesn't change the location that this pair of numbers is referring to.

00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:15.860
The pattern that we'll look at centers around plotting points where both of these coordinates are a given prime number.

00:01:16.540 --> 00:01:20.069
There is no practical reason to do this. It's purely fun.

00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:24.930
We're just frolicking around in the playground of data visualization and to get a sense for what it means

00:01:24.930 --> 00:01:28.020
Look at all the whole numbers rather than just the primes.

00:01:28.200 --> 00:01:33.900
The point (1, 1) sits a distance one away from the origin with an angle of 1 radian.

00:01:34.260 --> 00:01:37.720
Which actually means this arc is the same length as that radial line

00:01:37.720 --> 00:01:41.880
and then (2, 2) has twice that angle and twice the distance

00:01:42.189 --> 00:01:46.169
and to get to (3, 3) you rotate one more radian with a total angle

00:01:46.170 --> 00:01:49.799
that's now slightly less than a half turn since 3 is slightly less than pi

00:01:49.869 --> 00:01:52.469
and you step one unit farther away from the origin.

00:01:53.200 --> 00:01:56.009
I really want you to make sure that it's clear what's being plotted because

00:01:56.110 --> 00:01:58.319
everything that follows depends on understanding it.

00:01:58.380 --> 00:02:03.500
Each step forward is like the tip of a clock hand which rotates one radian with each tick

00:02:03.520 --> 00:02:04.920
(a little less than a sixth of a turn)

00:02:04.920 --> 00:02:07.200
and it grows by one unit at each step.

00:02:07.720 --> 00:02:11.680
As you continue these points spiral outwards forming

00:02:11.680 --> 00:02:14.180
what's known in the business as an Archimedean spiral.

00:02:14.980 --> 00:02:19.840
Now if you make the admittedly arbitrary move to knock out everything except the prime numbers

00:02:20.020 --> 00:02:25.409
It initially looks quite random after all primes are famous for their chaotic and difficult to predict behavior

00:02:25.960 --> 00:02:27.960
but when you zoom out

00:02:29.920 --> 00:02:37.440
What you start to see are these very clear galactic seeming spirals and what's weird is some of the arms seem to be missing.

00:02:39.300 --> 00:02:43.860
Then zooming out even further those spirals give way to a different pattern

00:02:52.120 --> 00:02:55.080
These many different outward pointing rays.

00:02:56.540 --> 00:02:59.620
And those rays seem to mostly come in clumps of four

00:03:01.159 --> 00:03:04.688
But there's the occasional gap like a comb missing its teeth.

00:03:06.290 --> 00:03:12.249
The question for you and me naturally is what on earth is going on here. Where do these spirals come from?

00:03:12.250 --> 00:03:15.279
And why do we instead get straight lines at this larger scale?

00:03:15.620 --> 00:03:21.039
If you wanted you could ask a more quantitative question and count that there are 20 total spirals

00:03:22.580 --> 00:03:28.640
And then up at that larger scale if you patiently went through each ray you'd count up a total of 280.

00:03:29.420 --> 00:03:34.740
And so this adds a further mystery of where these numbers are coming from and why they would arise from primes.

00:03:36.190 --> 00:03:42.269
Now this is shocking and beautiful and you might think that it suggests some divine hidden symmetry within the primes.

00:03:42.640 --> 00:03:45.360
But to steady your expectations I should say that

00:03:45.360 --> 00:03:51.820
the fact that the person asking this question on Math Exchange jumped right into prime numbers makes the puzzle a little misleading

00:03:52.540 --> 00:03:54.860
if you look at all of the whole numbers (not just the primes).

00:03:58.880 --> 00:04:02.380
As you zoom out you see very similar spirals

00:04:03.640 --> 00:04:07.800
They're much cleaner and now there's 44 of them instead of 20

00:04:08.280 --> 00:04:12.520
But it means that the question of where the spirals come from is perhaps disappointingly

00:04:12.880 --> 00:04:16.100
Completely separate from the question of what happens when we limit our view to primes.

00:04:17.660 --> 00:04:21.600
But don't be too disappointed because both these questions are still phenomenal puzzles.

00:04:21.600 --> 00:04:24.420
There's a very satisfying answer for the spirals and

00:04:24.520 --> 00:04:29.460
Even if the primes don't cause the spirals asking what goes on when you filter for those primes

00:04:29.620 --> 00:04:34.024
Does lead you to one of the most important theorems about the distribution of prime numbers

00:04:34.024 --> 00:04:36.299
known in number theory as Dirichlet's theorem.

00:04:37.780 --> 00:04:40.580
To kick things off. Let's zoom back in a little bit smaller.

00:04:41.240 --> 00:04:44.780
Did you notice that as we were zooming out there were these six little spirals?

00:04:45.260 --> 00:04:48.900
This offers a good starting point to explain what's happening in the two larger patterns.

00:04:49.300 --> 00:04:53.200
Notice how all the multiples of 6 form one arm of this spiral.

00:04:55.200 --> 00:04:59.200
Then the next one is every integer, that's 1 above a multiple of 6.

00:05:01.000 --> 00:05:06.280
Then after that it includes all the numbers 2 above a multiple of 6 and so on. Why is that?

00:05:08.320 --> 00:05:12.740
Well, remember that each step forward in the sequence involves a turn of one radian.

00:05:13.120 --> 00:05:16.500
So when you count up by 6, you've turned a total of 6 radians

00:05:16.500 --> 00:05:19.760
Which is a little bit less than 2π, a full turn.

00:05:20.280 --> 00:05:21.600
So every time you count up by 6

00:05:21.610 --> 00:05:24.699
You've almost made a full turn. It's just a little less.

00:05:25.490 --> 00:05:27.969
Another six steps, a slightly smaller angle.

00:05:29.030 --> 00:05:32.460
Six more steps, smaller still and so on.

00:05:32.460 --> 00:05:37.560
With this angle changing gently enough that it gives the illusion of a single curving line.

00:05:38.240 --> 00:05:42.460
When you limit the view to prime numbers all but two of these spiral arms

00:05:42.460 --> 00:05:45.280
they'll go away, and think about it.

00:05:45.280 --> 00:05:47.620
A prime number can't be a multiple of 6

00:05:48.080 --> 00:05:53.260
and it also can't be 2 above a multiple of 6 unless it's 2 or 4 above a multiple of 6

00:05:53.260 --> 00:05:55.220
since all of those are even numbers.

00:05:55.540 --> 00:06:01.800
It also can't be 3 above a multiple of 6 unless it's the number 3 itself since all of those are divisible by 3.

00:06:02.960 --> 00:06:06.540
So at least at the smaller-scale nothing magical is going on.

00:06:06.860 --> 00:06:08.940
And while we're in the simpler context

00:06:08.950 --> 00:06:11.760
let me introduce some terminology that mathematicians use.

00:06:11.760 --> 00:06:16.119
Each one of these sequences where you're counting up by 6 is fancifully called a

00:06:16.310 --> 00:06:18.310
"residue class mod 6".

00:06:18.740 --> 00:06:22.880
The word "residue" here is sort of an overdramatic way of saying remainder

00:06:23.160 --> 00:06:27.460
and mod means something like where the thing you divide by is.

00:06:28.240 --> 00:06:33.040
So for example 6 goes into 20 three times and it leaves a remainder of 2

00:06:34.300 --> 00:06:37.440
So 20 has a residue of 2 mod 6.

00:06:39.000 --> 00:06:44.160
Together with all the other numbers leaving a remainder of 2 when the thing you divide by is 6

00:06:44.160 --> 00:06:46.980
you have a full residue class mod 6

00:06:47.420 --> 00:06:52.580
I know that that sounds like the world's most pretentious way of saying "everything 2 above a multiple of 6".

00:06:52.950 --> 00:06:57.170
But this is the standard jargon and it is actually handy to have some words for the idea.

00:06:57.630 --> 00:07:04.279
So looking at our diagram in the lingo each of these spiral arms corresponds to a residue class mod 6

00:07:04.280 --> 00:07:10.130
and the reason we see them is that 6 is close to 2π turning 6 radians is almost a full turn.

00:07:10.400 --> 00:07:14.140
And the reason we see only two of them when filtering for primes is that

00:07:14.140 --> 00:07:19.420
all prime numbers are either 1 or 5 above a multiple of 6 with the exceptions of 2 and 3.

00:07:19.940 --> 00:07:25.720
With all that as a warm-up, let's think about the larger scale. In the same way that six steps is close to a full turn

00:07:26.280 --> 00:07:30.350
Taking 44 steps is very close to a whole number of turns.

00:07:31.080 --> 00:07:35.420
Let's compute it. There are 2π radians per rotation, right?

00:07:35.700 --> 00:07:42.460
So taking 44 steps, turning 44 radians, gives a total of 44 divided by 2π rotations,

00:07:42.460 --> 00:07:45.840
which comes out to be just barely above seven full turns.

00:07:46.380 --> 00:07:51.439
You could also write this by saying that 44/7 is a close approximation for 2π.

00:07:51.660 --> 00:07:55.999
Which some of you may better recognize as the famous 22/7 approximation for π.

00:07:57.120 --> 00:08:01.420
What this means is when you count up by multiples of 44 in the diagram

00:08:01.420 --> 00:08:05.800
Each point has almost the same angle as the last one just a little bit bigger.

00:08:06.480 --> 00:08:12.760
So as you continue on with more and more we get this very gentle spiral as the angle increases very slowly

00:08:14.700 --> 00:08:21.020
Similarly all the numbers 1 above a multiple of 44 make another spiral but rotated 1 radian counterclockwise.

00:08:21.560 --> 00:08:27.360
Same for everything 2 above a multiple of 44 and so on eventually filling out the full diagram.

00:08:28.140 --> 00:08:33.900
To phrase it with our fancier language, each of these spiral arms shows a residue class mod 44.

00:08:38.300 --> 00:08:42.280
And maybe now you can tell me what happens when we limit our view to prime numbers.

00:08:42.960 --> 00:08:46.540
Primes cannot be a multiple of 44 so that arm won't be visible

00:08:46.920 --> 00:08:51.160
nor can a prime be 2 above a multiple of 44 or 4 above and so on

00:08:51.160 --> 00:08:54.220
since all those residue classes have nothing but even numbers.

00:08:57.060 --> 00:09:01.320
Likewise any multiples of 11 can't be prime except for 11 itself.

00:09:01.510 --> 00:09:04.920
So the spiral of numbers 11 above a multiple of 44

00:09:04.920 --> 00:09:09.719
They won't be visible. And neither will the spiral of numbers 33 above a multiple of 44.

00:09:10.150 --> 00:09:13.900
This is what gives the picture those Milky Way seeming gaps.

00:09:13.900 --> 00:09:19.260
Each spiral were left with is a residue class that doesn't share any prime factors with 44.

00:09:19.860 --> 00:09:22.740
And within each one of those arms that we can't reject out of hand

00:09:22.740 --> 00:09:28.020
The prime numbers seem to be sort of randomly distributed and that's a fact that I'd like you to tuck away.

00:09:28.020 --> 00:09:29.960
We'll return to it later.

00:09:30.360 --> 00:09:34.400
This is another good chance to inject some of the jargon that mathematicians use.

00:09:34.780 --> 00:09:40.620
what we care about right here are all the numbers between 0 and 43 that don't share a prime factor with 44, right?

00:09:40.920 --> 00:09:44.140
The ones that aren't even and they also aren't divisible by 11.

00:09:44.800 --> 00:09:47.220
When two numbers don't share any factors like this

00:09:48.060 --> 00:09:52.500
We call them "relatively prime" or also "coprime".

00:09:52.700 --> 00:09:58.860
In this example, you could count that there are 20 different numbers between 1 and 44 that are coprime to 44

00:09:59.520 --> 00:10:03.200
And this is a fact that a number theorist would compactly write by saying:

00:10:03.200 --> 00:10:05.240
phi of 44 equals 20.

00:10:05.440 --> 00:10:11.200
Where the Greek letter phi here refers to Euler's totient function, yet another needlessly fancy word

00:10:11.740 --> 00:10:16.980
Which is defined to be the number of integers from 1 up to n which are coprime to n.

00:10:17.820 --> 00:10:20.540
It comes up enough that it's handy to have compact notation.

00:10:20.920 --> 00:10:25.040
More obscurely and I had never heard this before but I find it too delightful not to tell

00:10:25.460 --> 00:10:28.560
these numbers are sometimes called the "totatives" of n.

00:10:29.500 --> 00:10:33.380
Back to the main thread in short what the user on math exchange was seeing

00:10:33.380 --> 00:10:37.080
are two unrelated pieces of number theory but illustrated in one drawing.

00:10:37.620 --> 00:10:42.860
The first is that 44/7 is a very close rational approximation for 2π

00:10:43.060 --> 00:10:47.680
Which results in the residue classes mod 44 being cleanly separated out.

00:10:48.600 --> 00:10:54.920
The second is that many of these residue classes contain zero prime numbers or sometimes just one so they won't show up.

00:10:55.300 --> 00:10:57.400
But on the other hand primes do show up

00:10:57.420 --> 00:11:02.760
plentifully enough in all 20 of the other residue classes that it makes these spiral arms visible.

00:11:03.840 --> 00:11:07.500
And at this point maybe you can predict what's going on at the larger scale.

00:11:08.440 --> 00:11:15.020
Just as 6 radians is vaguely close to a full turn and 44 radians is quite close to seven full turns

00:11:15.440 --> 00:11:18.520
it just so happens that 710 radians is

00:11:18.580 --> 00:11:21.280
extremely close to a whole number of full turns.

00:11:21.780 --> 00:11:27.980
Visually, you can see this by the fact that the point ends up almost exactly on the x axis, but it's more compelling analytically.

00:11:28.740 --> 00:11:34.220
710 radians is 710 divided by 2π rotations, which works out to be

00:11:34.840 --> 00:11:39.420
113 point 0 0 0 0 0 9 5.

00:11:40.260 --> 00:11:42.400
Some of you may have seen this in another form.

00:11:42.700 --> 00:11:49.460
It's saying the 710/113 is a close approximation for 2π which is more commonly seen in saying that

00:11:49.460 --> 00:11:54.120
355/113 is a very good approximation for π.

00:11:55.640 --> 00:12:01.160
Now if you want to understand where these rational approximations are coming from and what it means for one like this to be

00:12:01.360 --> 00:12:07.420
Unusually good, like way better than you would get for Φ or e or √2 or other famous irrationals.

00:12:07.420 --> 00:12:10.720
I highly recommend taking a look at this great Mathologer video.

00:12:11.580 --> 00:12:16.000
For our storyline though. It means that when you move forward by steps of 710

00:12:16.020 --> 00:12:20.760
the angle of each new point is almost exactly the same as the last one

00:12:20.760 --> 00:12:22.620
Just microscopically bigger.

00:12:24.720 --> 00:12:28.319
Even very far out one of these sequences looks like a straight line.

00:12:29.300 --> 00:12:34.320
And of course, the other residue classes mod 710 also form these nearly straight lines.

00:12:35.580 --> 00:12:40.540
710 is a big number though. So when all of them are on-screen and there's only so many pixels on the screen

00:12:40.780 --> 00:12:42.420
It's a little hard to make them out.

00:12:43.980 --> 00:12:47.940
So in this case it's actually easier to see when we limit the view to primes

00:12:47.940 --> 00:12:50.440
where you don't see many of those residue classes.

00:12:51.300 --> 00:12:57.120
In reality with a little further zooming you can see that there actually is a very gentle spiral to these.

00:12:57.460 --> 00:13:01.300
But the fact that it takes so long to become prominent is a wonderful illustration.

00:13:01.300 --> 00:13:06.400
Maybe the best illustration I've ever seen for just how good an approximation this is for 2π.

00:13:08.240 --> 00:13:12.840
Tying up the remaining loose thread here if you want to understand what happens when you filter for primes

00:13:12.880 --> 00:13:15.880
It's entirely analogous to what we did before.

00:13:15.880 --> 00:13:20.160
The factors of 710 are 71, 5 and 2.

00:13:20.360 --> 00:13:25.720
So if the remainder or residue is divisible by any of those then so is the number.

00:13:26.480 --> 00:13:29.520
When you pull up all of the residue classes with odd numbers

00:13:31.900 --> 00:13:35.700
It looks like every other ray in the otherwise quite crowded picture

00:13:38.900 --> 00:13:43.540
And then of those that remain these are the ones that are divisible by 5

00:13:43.540 --> 00:13:46.480
Which are nice and evenly spaced at every fifth line.

00:13:49.220 --> 00:13:52.420
Notice, the fact that prime numbers never show up in any of these

00:13:52.420 --> 00:13:56.580
is what explains the pattern of the lines we saw at the beginning coming in clumps of four.

00:13:57.160 --> 00:14:03.260
And moreover, of those remaining these four residue classes are the ones that are divisible by 71

00:14:03.660 --> 00:14:05.880
So the primes aren't going to show up there.

00:14:05.880 --> 00:14:11.300
And that's what explains why the clumps of four that we saw occasionally have like a missing tooth in your cone.

00:14:13.720 --> 00:14:16.880
And if you were wondering where that number 280 came from?

00:14:17.200 --> 00:14:23.760
It comes from counting how many of the numbers from 1 up to 710 don't share any prime factors with 710.

00:14:24.200 --> 00:14:29.240
These are the ones that we can't rule out for including primes based on some obvious divisibility consideration.

00:14:30.240 --> 00:14:34.760
This of course doesn't guarantee that any particular one will contain prime numbers

00:14:35.320 --> 00:14:37.440
But at least empirically when you look at this picture

00:14:37.660 --> 00:14:42.000
It actually seems like the primes are pretty evenly distributed among the remaining classes.

00:14:42.000 --> 00:14:43.040
Wouldn't you agree?

00:14:47.440 --> 00:14:50.800
This last point is actually the most interesting observation of the whole deal.

00:14:50.800 --> 00:14:54.780
It relates to a pretty deep fact in number theory known as Dirichlet's theorem.

00:14:55.400 --> 00:15:00.400
To take a simpler example than residue classes mod 710 think of those mod 10.

00:15:00.860 --> 00:15:06.420
Because we write numbers in base 10, this is the same thing as grouping numbers together by what their last digit is.

00:15:07.020 --> 00:15:12.740
Everything whose last digit is 0 is a residue class, everything whose last digit is 1 is another residue class and so on.

00:15:13.580 --> 00:15:18.240
Other than two prime numbers can't have an even number as their last digit since that means they're even

00:15:19.020 --> 00:15:22.300
And likewise any prime bigger than 5 can't end in a 5.

00:15:22.300 --> 00:15:26.660
There's nothing surprising there, that's one of the first facts you observe when you learn about prime numbers.

00:15:26.660 --> 00:15:31.440
Anything bigger than 5 has to end in either a 1, a 3, a 7, or a 9.

00:15:31.900 --> 00:15:33.900
A much more nuanced question though

00:15:33.900 --> 00:15:37.820
is how exactly these primes are divvied up among those remaining four groups.

00:15:38.320 --> 00:15:42.120
Here, let's make a quick histogram counting through each prime number

00:15:42.120 --> 00:15:48.020
where the bars are going to show what proportion of the primes that we've seen so far have a given last digit.

00:15:48.500 --> 00:15:52.220
So in particular the 2 and the 5 slots should go down to 0 over time.

00:15:52.860 --> 00:15:56.840
What would you predict is gonna happen as we move through more and more primes?

00:16:02.020 --> 00:16:02.840
Well

00:16:03.320 --> 00:16:09.040
As we get a lot of them, it seems like a pretty even spread between these four classes about 25% each.

00:16:09.300 --> 00:16:11.300
And probably that's what you would expect.

00:16:11.300 --> 00:16:16.220
After all, why would prime numbers show some sort of preference for one last digit over another?

00:16:16.560 --> 00:16:18.120
But Prime's aren't random

00:16:18.130 --> 00:16:21.300
they are a definite sequence and they show patterns in other ways

00:16:21.300 --> 00:16:24.380
and it's highly non-obvious how you would prove something like this.

00:16:24.700 --> 00:16:29.140
Or for that matter, how do you rigorously phrase what it is that you want to prove?

00:16:29.720 --> 00:16:32.020
A mathematician might go about it something like this:

00:16:32.540 --> 00:16:35.940
If you look at all the prime numbers less than some big number x

00:16:36.400 --> 00:16:40.900
and you consider what fraction of them are, say, 1 above a multiple of 10

00:16:41.980 --> 00:16:46.720
That fraction should approach 1/4 as x approaches infinity.

00:16:48.220 --> 00:16:52.920
And likewise for all of the other allowable residue classes like 3 and 7 and 9.

00:16:54.880 --> 00:16:59.480
Of course there's nothing special about 10 - a similar fact should hold for any other number.

00:16:59.640 --> 00:17:03.920
Considering our old friends the residue classes mod 44, for example,

00:17:03.920 --> 00:17:08.820
let's make a similar histogram showing what proportion of the primes show up in each one of these.

00:17:17.520 --> 00:17:23.820
Again as time goes on we see an even spread between the 20 different allowable residue classes.

00:17:23.880 --> 00:17:27.280
Which you can think of in terms of each spiral arm from our diagram

00:17:27.520 --> 00:17:30.240
Having about the same number of primes as each of the others.

00:17:31.120 --> 00:17:34.980
Maybe that's what you would expect but this is a shockingly hard fact to prove.

00:17:36.940 --> 00:17:42.340
The first man who cracked this puzzle was Dirichlet, in 1837.

00:17:42.340 --> 00:17:46.000
And it forms one of the crowning jewels at the foundation of modern analytic number theory.

00:17:47.140 --> 00:17:51.380
Histograms like these ones give a pretty good illustration of what the theorem is actually saying.

00:17:51.380 --> 00:17:55.660
Nevertheless you might find it enlightening to see how it might be written in a math text

00:17:55.660 --> 00:17:57.420
with all the fancy jargon and everything.

00:17:57.840 --> 00:18:01.000
It's essentially what we just saw for 10, but more general.

00:18:01.320 --> 00:18:04.200
Again, you look at all of the primes up to some bound x

00:18:04.510 --> 00:18:09.719
But instead of asking for what proportion of them have a residue of, say, 1 mod 10

00:18:09.970 --> 00:18:13.500
You ask what proportion have a residue of r mod n,

00:18:13.800 --> 00:18:18.840
Where n is any number and r is anything that's coprime to n.

00:18:19.120 --> 00:18:22.400
Remember, that means it doesn't share any factors with n bigger than 1.

00:18:23.020 --> 00:18:26.060
Instead of approaching 1/4 as x goes to infinity,

00:18:26.380 --> 00:18:29.660
that proportion goes to 1 divided by phi of n,

00:18:29.660 --> 00:18:35.760
where phi is that special function I mentioned earlier that gives the number of possible residues coprime to n.

00:18:36.370 --> 00:18:40.320
And in case this is too clear for the reader  you might see it buried in more notation.

00:18:40.320 --> 00:18:44.860
Where this denominator and the numerator are both written with a special prime counting function.

00:18:45.280 --> 00:18:49.600
The convention, rather confusingly, is to use the symbol π for this function,

00:18:49.920 --> 00:18:52.740
even though it's totally unrelated to the number pi.

00:18:53.260 --> 00:18:58.460
In some contexts when people refer to Dirichlet's Theorem, they refer to a much more modest statement.

00:18:58.500 --> 00:19:04.560
Which is simply that each of these residue classes that MIGHT have infinitely many primes DOES have infinitely many.

00:19:04.920 --> 00:19:07.600
In order to prove this, what Dirichlet did

00:19:07.600 --> 00:19:12.480
was show that the primes are just as dense in any one of these residue classes as in any other.

00:19:13.360 --> 00:19:18.420
For example, imagine someone asked you to prove that there are infinitely many primes ending in the number 1,

00:19:18.420 --> 00:19:22.560
and the way you do it is by showing that a quarter of all the primes end in a 1.

00:19:23.120 --> 00:19:26.880
Together with the fact that there are infinitely many primes, which we've known since Euclid,

00:19:27.280 --> 00:19:30.320
This gives a stronger statement and a much more interesting one.

00:19:31.740 --> 00:19:37.240
Now the proof - well, it's way more involved than would be reasonable to show here.

00:19:37.240 --> 00:19:41.849
One interesting fact worth mentioning is that it relies heavily on complex analysis.

00:19:41.950 --> 00:19:47.260
Which is the study of doing calculus with functions whose inputs and outputs are complex numbers.

00:19:47.260 --> 00:19:49.260
Now that might seem weird, right?

00:19:49.410 --> 00:19:53.540
I mean, prime numbers seem wholly unrelated to the continuous world of calculus;

00:19:53.540 --> 00:19:56.060
Much less when complex numbers end up in the mix.

00:19:56.420 --> 00:19:57.960
But since the early 19th century

00:19:57.960 --> 00:20:02.840
This is absolutely par for the course, when it comes to understanding how primes are distributed.

00:20:03.460 --> 00:20:05.780
And this isn't just antiquated technology either

00:20:05.820 --> 00:20:08.980
Understanding the distribution of primes in residue classes like this

00:20:09.280 --> 00:20:11.920
Continues to be relevant in modern research too!

00:20:11.920 --> 00:20:14.700
Some of the recent breakthroughs on small gaps between primes,

00:20:14.940 --> 00:20:17.820
edging towards that ever elusive twin prime conjecture,

00:20:18.120 --> 00:20:23.200
have their basis in understanding how primes split up among these kinds of residue classes.

00:20:27.240 --> 00:20:30.660
Okay, looking back over the puzzle, I want to emphasize something

00:20:31.200 --> 00:20:35.280
The original bit of data visualization whimsy that led to these patterns

00:20:35.500 --> 00:20:38.020
Well, like it doesn't matter. No one cares

00:20:38.020 --> 00:20:41.640
There's nothing special about plotting (p, p) in polar coordinates.

00:20:41.940 --> 00:20:44.400
And most of the initial mystery in these spirals

00:20:44.400 --> 00:20:49.340
resulted from the artifacts that come from dealing with integer number of radians, which is kind of weird.

00:20:49.880 --> 00:20:53.540
But on the other hand, this kind of play is CLEARLY worth it

00:20:53.540 --> 00:20:59.260
If the end result is a line of questions that leads you to something like Dirichlet's theorem, which is important.

00:20:59.280 --> 00:21:04.080
Especially if it  inspires you to learn enough to understand the tactics of the underlying proof.

00:21:04.440 --> 00:21:06.040
No small task, by the way.

00:21:07.440 --> 00:21:13.540
And this isn't a coincidence that a fairly random question like this can lead you to an important and deep fact for math.

00:21:13.600 --> 00:21:19.640
What it means for a piece of math to be important and deep is that it connects to many other topics.

00:21:20.540 --> 00:21:23.120
So even an arbitrary exploration of numbers

00:21:23.120 --> 00:21:25.120
as long as it's not too arbitrary

00:21:25.120 --> 00:21:27.900
Has a good chance of stumbling into something meaningful.

00:21:29.320 --> 00:21:34.880
Sure, you'll get a much more concentrated dosage of important facts by going through a textbook or a course.

00:21:35.340 --> 00:21:37.500
And there will be many fewer uninteresting dead ends.

00:21:38.100 --> 00:21:41.300
But there is something special about rediscovering these topics on your own.

00:21:41.760 --> 00:21:46.040
If you effectively reinvent Euler's totient function, before you've ever seen it defined

00:21:46.360 --> 00:21:50.720
Or if you start wondering about rational approximations before learning about continued fractions

00:21:51.160 --> 00:21:54.860
Or if you seriously explore how primes are divvied up between residue classes

00:21:54.860 --> 00:21:57.200
Before you've even heard the name "Dirichlet"

00:21:57.580 --> 00:21:59.520
Then when you DO learn those topics,

00:21:59.660 --> 00:22:02.800
you'll see them as familiar friends, not as arbitrary definitions,

00:22:02.800 --> 00:22:06.300
and that will almost certainly mean that you learn it more effectively.

